\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Irregular time-series forecasting is commonly treated as a setting that requires
specialized neural architectures for handling uneven timestamps, missingness,
and variable-specific observation patterns. This paper asks a deliberately
simple question: are neural models necessary for the standard irregular
forecasting benchmarks? We introduce an \family{} of deterministic, gradient-free
history anchors and a unified selector, \method{}, evaluated under the same
splits, scaling, masks, and metrics as the APN benchmark protocol. Results on PhysioNet, MIMIC-III, USHCN, and HumanActivity
show that \method{} matches or exceeds APN-style reported performance on
several metrics while requiring no backpropagation and no GPU training. The goal of the paper is not to claim that neural models are never
useful, but to show that current benchmark conclusions are incomplete unless
strong non-neural anchor baselines are included.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Irregular multivariate time series (IMTS) forecasting is often viewed as a problem that
demands specialized neural architectures. Unlike regularly sampled sequences,
irregular time series contain uneven observation times, variable-specific
missingness, sparse histories, and partially observed future targets. These
properties have motivated a large body of neural methods based on attention,
latent dynamics, temporal point processes, graph message passing, interpolation,
and adaptive patching. Recent work such as APN \citep{apn} further argues that carefully
designed neural aggregation (patching) can produce strong performance on standard irregular
forecasting benchmarks \citep{apn}.

In this paper, we ask a simpler question: \emph{are neural models necessary for
irregular time-series forecasting?} Our motivation follows the broader lesson of
\citet{linear}, who showed that strong linear baselines can overturn
the perceived advantage of complex Transformer architectures in long-horizon
time-series forecasting. We revisit the same type of question in the irregular
setting. If much of the target signal is locally stable, repeated, or well
explained by recent history, then a complex neural model may succeed partly
because the benchmark rewards anchoring to observed values, rather than because
it learns rich irregular dynamics. To test this hypothesis, we introduce the \emph{Anchor family}, a set of deterministic, non-neural rules for irregular time-series forecasting: NaiveAnchor (last-value repetition), ExpoAnchor (exponential smoothing), SparseAnchor (sparse and repeated-value histories), ERMAnchor (non-test empirical-risk selection), and AutoAnchor (empirical selection with generic history-structure priors). These methods use observed values and timestamps without neural parameters, backpropagation, or GPU training. The resulting baseline is intentionally and embarrassingly straightforward. Given a variable-specific
history
\[
    \mathcal{H}_v = \{(t_{v,j}, x_{v,j})\}_{j=1}^{n_v},
\]
an anchor method forecasts future targets by selecting a history-derived
reference value,
\[
    \hat{y}_{b,k,v} = f_{\theta_v}(\mathcal{H}_{b,v}, k),
\]
Accordingly, $f_{\theta_v}$ is either a fixed anchor rule or a candidate selected from a
shared library before test evaluation. This formulation makes every prediction
traceable to an explicit rule, in contrast to learned latent representations or
black-box sequence encoders. Empirically, the Anchor family surprisingly dominates. Benchmarking on the renowned
PhysioNet P12, USHCN, HumanActivity, and MIMIC datasets, AutoAnchor
outperforms the reported APN results under the same masked scaled evaluation
protocol, while using zero neural parameters and zero GPU memory. The result does
not imply that neural models are barely useful for irregular forecasting. Rather,
it shows that current benchmark conclusions are incomplete unless they include
strong deterministic statistical forecasting baselines that measure how much performance is explained by local
history alone, shifting the emphasis from architecture-first novelty to data-first understanding for asynchronous time series. 

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose the Anchor family, a transparent set of gradient-free
    forecasting baselines for irregular multivariate time series, in which AutoAnchor holds a unified leading position.
    \item We introduce AutoAnchor, a dynamic anchor selector that uses non-test
    empirical risk and generic history-structure priors without dataset-specific
    or variable-specific lookup tables.
    \item We show that simple anchors can outperform state-of-the-art neural
    irregular forecasting models on public benchmark datasets that span domains (e.g., ICU, Motion, Climate).
    \item The Anchor family serves as a compulsory diagnostic baseline before accepting claims that models learn meaningful irregular temporal dynamics in forecasting benchmarks.
\end{itemize}

\section{Background and Motivation}

\paragraph{From missingness-aware models to continuous-time representations.}
Early IMTS methods treated observation masks, elapsed time, and asynchronous
measurements as first-class inputs. GRU-D~\citep{GRUD} uses missingness
indicators and temporal decay in recurrent updates, while mTAN~\citep{mTAN}
learns continuous-time attention features. Set-based encoders such as
SeFT~\citep{SeFT} avoid forcing irregular events onto a regular grid, and
Raindrop~\citep{Raindrop} represents sensor dependencies with a learned graph.
More recent models have moved toward continuous-time attention and
sample-centric interaction modeling. ContiFormer~\citep{ContiFormer2023}
combines Transformer attention with Neural ODE-style continuous dynamics,
whereas CoFormer~\citep{CoFormer2023} treats each observation as a
variable--time point and learns intra-variable and inter-variable
relationships directly. Rough Transformers~\citep{RoughTransformer2024}
further use path-signature representations to capture multi-scale continuous
dynamics with lower computational cost. This progression shows that the field
has steadily expanded the learned representation used to handle irregularity;
it does not yet establish how much of benchmark performance requires that
additional representation.

\paragraph{Recent forecasting architectures.}
For forecasting specifically, NeuralFlows~\citep{NeuralFlows}, CRU~\citep{CRU},
and GNeuralFlow~\citep{GNeuralFlow} model latent continuous-time dynamics,
while tPatchGNN~\citep{tPatchGNN} and GraFITi~\citep{GraFITi} use patching and
graph structure to model asynchronous variables. TimeCHEAT~\citep{TimeCHEAT2025}
combines local channel-dependent processing with global channel-independent
attention. The most recent IMTS models increasingly target multi-scale and
higher-order interactions: Hi-Patch~\citep{HiPatch2025} builds hierarchical
patch graphs, HyperIMTS~\citep{HyperIMTS2025} uses temporal and variable
hyperedges, and KAFNet~\citep{KAFNet2026} revisits canonical pre-alignment with
learned smoothing, compression, and frequency-domain cross-variable modeling.
These approaches address real limitations of irregular forecasting, including
inter-variable asynchrony, different sampling scales, and long-range
dependencies. They also make the need for a strong low-complexity control more
pressing, because additional architectural capacity should be credited only
for predictive information that is unavailable from the observed history.

\paragraph{The recent efficiency turn.}
Our motivation is closely related to the recent APN paper, whose title
explicitly argues for a simple and effective irregular-forecasting baseline
\citep{apn}. APN uses time-aware adaptive patch aggregation, a query module,
and a shallow MLP, and therefore provides an important efficiency-oriented
neural reference point~\citep{SimpleTM2025}. It is nevertheless a learned model: its patch
boundaries, representations, and prediction function are trained from data.
APN consequently does not answer the more basic control question studied here:
how much performance can be obtained with no gradient updates, no learned
representation, and no GPU training? AnchorFamily complements rather than
duplicates APN by isolating the contribution of explicit local history rules
from the contribution of trainable adaptive aggregation.

\paragraph{Simple baselines as scientific controls.}
This distinction follows a broader lesson in time-series evaluation. Strong linear, statistical, and lightweight controls can narrow or reverse
the apparent advantage of complex architectures when the target is locally persistent or
smooth~\citep{linear,Zeng2023LTSFLinear,SimpleTM2025,TFB2024}. In IMTS, the corresponding controls include
last-value repetition, exponential smoothing, sparse-history repetition, local
trend extrapolation, and phase-like rules. A single persistence baseline is not
enough: it may fail because the history is sparse, repeated, or locally
oscillatory, rather than because a neural model has learned meaningful
irregular dynamics. A useful diagnostic baseline must therefore cover several
plausible history structures while remaining transparent and free of
dataset-specific lookup tables.

\paragraph{The benchmarking gap and our position.}
Recent IMTS work has substantially improved the modeling of continuous time,
cross-variable structure, and computational efficiency, but comparisons still
primarily ask which learned architecture performs best~\citep{TFB2024,TimeRecipe2026}. What is less
consistently tested is whether those gains survive a strong deterministic
history-only control evaluated under the same split, masking, scaling, and
target-availability rules~\citep{TFB2024,TimeRecipe2026}. We introduce the Anchor family to fill this gap.
AutoAnchor selects among explicit candidates using non-test empirical risk and
generic history-structure diagnostics, then freezes the selected rule before
test evaluation. The resulting comparison is deliberately narrower and more
falsifiable than a universal claim about neural models: it measures what
learned IMTS architectures add beyond local stability, sparse repetition,
smoothing, and short-range temporal structure, while leaving regime changes,
delayed effects, phase reversals, and hidden cross-variable dynamics as
settings in which neural models may provide genuine additional value.

\section{The Anchor Family}
% BEGIN ANCHOR-FAMILY METHOD SECTION

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Notation used in the Anchor family.}
\label{tab:anchor-notation}
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{0.92}
\begin{tabular}{p{0.22\linewidth}p{0.72\linewidth}}
\toprule
Symbol & Meaning \\
\midrule
$b,v,j,k$ & Sample, variable, history, and forecast indices. \\
$\mathcal H_{b,v}$ & Irregular history $\{(t_j,x_j)\}_{j=1}^{n_{b,v}}$. \\
$L,K,t^*_{b,k,v}$ & Lookback, horizon, and future query time. \\
$S_v,\widetilde x$ & Benchmark scaler and scaled value $S_v(x)$. \\
$\mathcal D_{\mathrm{cal}},m_{b,k,v}$ & Pooled train--validation set and target-observation mask. \\
$\mathcal G,\mathcal A,f_a$ & Primitive set, 85-candidate library, and candidate forecast. \\
$z_i,\widetilde y_i$ & Candidate prediction and target in benchmark scale. \\
$\theta_v=(a_v,\beta_v)$ & Selected candidate and scalar coefficient for variable $v$. \\
$\Pi_{b,v},\widehat R_v$ & Recent-history projection and masked calibration risk. \\
$p_v,u_v,g_{\mathrm{ph}}$ & Modal fraction, distinct count, and $g_{\mathrm{phase},1/3,5}$. \\
$\Delta_{PL,v},\Delta_{PT,v}$ & Mean phase--last and phase--trend disagreement. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We introduce the Anchor Family, a suite of deterministic, gradient-free methods designed to quantify how much irregular time-series forecasting performance can be explained by explicit history-derived rules before attributing gains to learned temporal representations. Every family member selects one variable-level specification
$\theta_v=(a_v,\beta_v)$ on $\mathcal D_{\mathrm{cal}}$, freezes it before
testing, and reuses the same forecast operator. The variants are alternative
selection policies, rather than an ensemble or per-sample router
(Figure~\ref{fig:anchor-family-architecture}).

\IfFileExists{figures/anchor_family_figure.tex}{\input{figures/anchor_family_figure.tex}}{}

\paragraph{Candidate space.}
The shared primitive set is
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal G=\{\mathrm{last},\mathrm{mean}_{2,3,5,8},
 \mathrm{trim}_{3,5,8},\mathrm{mode},\mathrm{EMA}_{0.1:0.1:0.9},
 \mathrm{trend}_{3,5,8,12},\mathrm{phase}_{(1/4,3),(1/3,5),(1/2,5),(1,7)}\}.
 \label{eq:anchor-primitives}
\end{equation}
Level and EMA operators return constants over query times. Trend is a bounded
least-squares extrapolation from the last $r$ observations. For phase pair
$(\rho,q)$, set $P=\rho L$, compute
$d_j=|((t_j-t^*+P/2)\bmod P)-P/2|$, and average the $q$ nearest observations
with weights $(d_j+0.25)^{-1}$. Thus the period is prescribed by lookback $L$,
not learned from targets. Candidate $a$ is either a primitive or
\begin{equation}
 f_a(\mathcal H,t^*)=\sum_{\ell=1}^{J_a}w_{a,\ell}
 g_{a,\ell}(\mathcal H,t^*),\qquad w_{a,\ell}\geq0,\quad\sum_\ell w_{a,\ell}=1.
 \label{eq:anchor-candidate}
\end{equation}
The fixed library $\mathcal A$ contains 26 primitives, 40 two-way mixtures over
last, phase, trend, and $\mathrm{EMA}_{0.3}$, and 19 phase--trend--last mixtures:
85 candidates shared across all variables and datasets.

\paragraph{Calibration.}
Each candidate compares identity scaling with the closed-form proposal
\begin{equation}
 \beta^{\mathrm{LS}}_{a,v}=\operatorname{clip}\!\left(
 \frac{\sum_i z_i\widetilde y_i}{\sum_i z_i^2};0,1.25\right),
 \qquad\mathcal B_{a,v}=\{1,\beta^{\mathrm{LS}}_{a,v}\}.
 \label{eq:anchor-beta}
\end{equation}
$\Pi_{b,v}$ clips raw predictions to
$\mu(R_{b,v})\pm3\max\{\sigma(R_{b,v}),0.1\}$, where $R_{b,v}$ is the last ten
observations. Candidate selection minimizes the masked, postprocessed risk
\begin{equation}
 \widehat R_v(a,\beta)=
 \frac{\sum_{b,k}m_{b,k,v}\left[\widetilde y_{b,k,v}-
 S_v\!\left(\Pi_{b,v}\!\left[S_v^{-1}\!\left(
 \beta f_a(\mathcal H_{b,v},t^*_{b,k,v})\right)\right]\right)\right]^2}
 {\sum_{b,k}m_{b,k,v}},\qquad b\in\mathcal D_{\mathrm{cal}}.
 \label{eq:anchor-risk}
\end{equation}
After selection, test inference replaces $(a,\beta)$ in the inner expression by
$(a_v,\beta_v)$; no library search is repeated.

\paragraph{Five policies.}
NaiveAnchor fixes $\theta_v=(g_{\mathrm{last}},1)$. ExpoAnchor fixes
$\theta_v=(g_{\mathrm{EMA},\alpha(L,K)},1)$ with
$\alpha(L,K)=\operatorname{clip}(0.1\operatorname{round}(10\sqrt{K/L});0.1,0.9)$.
SparseAnchor applies the following ordered rule to four-decimal raw
calibration values:
\begin{equation}
 a_v^{\mathrm S}=\begin{cases}
 g_{\mathrm{mode}},&p_v>0.95\ \text{or}\ (p_v>0.50\wedge u_v>150),\\
 0.8g_{\mathrm{last}}+0.2g_{\mathrm{ph}},&p_v>0.50,\\
 g_{\mathrm{last}},&\text{otherwise},
 \end{cases}\qquad \theta_v^{\mathrm{sparse}}=(a_v^{\mathrm S},1).
 \label{eq:sparse-policy}
\end{equation}
ERMAnchor selects
$\theta_v^{\mathrm{ERM}}=\arg\min_{a\in\mathcal A,\beta\in\mathcal B_{a,v}}
\widehat R_v(a,\beta)$. Following, AutoAnchor starts from this ERM solution. The disagreement statistics use
calibration histories and query times, but no target values. The first
applicable structural rule returns
\begin{equation}
 a_v^{\mathrm P}=\begin{cases}
 g_{\mathrm{mode}},&p_v>0.95\ \text{or}\ (p_v>0.50\wedge u_v>150),\\
 0.8g_{\mathrm{last}}+0.2g_{\mathrm{ph}},&p_v>0.50,\\
 g_{\mathrm{EMA},\alpha(L,K)},&L\geq1000\ \wedge\ K/L\leq0.20,\\
 0.65g_{\mathrm{ph}}+0.25g_{\mathrm{trend},8}+0.10g_{\mathrm{last}},
 &L\geq100\ \wedge\Delta_{PT,v}\leq\Delta_{PL,v},\\
 0.75g_{\mathrm{ph}}+0.25g_{\mathrm{last}},&L\geq100,\\
 \varnothing,&\text{otherwise}.
 \end{cases}
 \label{eq:auto-prior}
\end{equation}
Finally, $\theta_v^{\mathrm{auto}}=(a_v^{\mathrm P},1)$ if a rule fires and
$\theta_v^{\mathrm{auto}}=\theta_v^{\mathrm{ERM}}$ otherwise. This is a hard,
dataset-agnostic structural override, not a learned gate. Primitive forecasts
are cached, so calibration scoring is $\mathcal O(85M_v)$ for $M_v$ valid
queries; all methods require neither backpropagation nor GPU parameters.

% END ANCHOR-FAMILY METHOD SECTION

\section{Environment Setup}
\subsection{Problem Formulation}

We consider irregular multivariate time-series forecasting. For each sample, the
observed history is a set of timestamped measurements, where $t_i \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ is the observation time,
$v_i \in \{1,\dots,V\}$ indexes the measured variable, and
$x_i \in \mathbb{R}$ is the observed value. For a specific variable $v$, we write
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}
    =
    \left\{
        (t_i, v_i, x_i)
    \right\}_{i=1}^{N},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}_v
    =
    \left\{
        (t_{v,j}, x_{v,j})
    \right\}_{j=1}^{n_v},
    \qquad
    t_{v,1} < \cdots < t_{v,n_v}.
\end{equation}
Given $\mathcal{H}$, the goal is to predict the next $K$ target values for each
evaluated variable. Let $y_{b,k,v}$ denote the ground-truth value for sample
$b$, horizon index $k$, and variable $v$, and let $\hat{y}_{b,k,v}$ be the
corresponding prediction. Since future observations are not always available,
we evaluate only observed targets using a binary mask
$m_{b,k,v} \in \{0,1\}$. The Anchor family forecasts by selecting a history-derived reference value, where $f_{\theta_v}$ is either a fixed anchor rule or a selected member of a
shared anchor library. This formulation uses values and timestamps, but does not
introduce neural parameters or gradient-based optimization:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{y}_{b,k,v}
    =
    f_{\theta_v}(\mathcal{H}_{b,v}, k),
\end{equation}

\subsection{Data Collection}

\paragraph{Benchmark composition.}
We evaluate on four real-world irregular time-series benchmarks spanning
clinical monitoring, biomechanics, and climate observation. PhysioNet P12
contains 36 time-varying ICU variables in the benchmark representation
\citep{Silva2012PhysioNet}. MIMIC-III contains 96 clinical variables derived
from de-identified critical-care records \citep{Johnson2016MIMIC}. HumanActivity
contains 12 three-dimensional positional variables recorded by four wearable
sensors during human activities \citep{Vidulin2010HumanActivity}. USHCN contains
five climate variables measured at weather stations across the United States
\citep{Menne2012USHCN}. Together, these datasets provide clinical, motion, and
climate settings with substantially different observation densities,
variable-specific missingness patterns, and temporal scales.

\paragraph{Observation format and preprocessing.}
The public datasets are obtained through the benchmark data-loading pipeline,
whereas MIMIC-III requires credentialed access and is processed from the
original clinical records. We retain the benchmark's dataset-specific
preprocessing and represent every sample as timestamped variable measurements
rather than as a fully observed regular grid. A sample therefore contains an
irregular history
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}
    =
    \{(t_i, v_i, x_i)\}_{i=1}^{N},
\end{equation}
where $t_i$ is the observation time, $v_i$ is the variable identity, and $x_i$
is the measured value.

\paragraph{Observed targets.}
The target window contains future observations together with a binary mask
indicating which values are actually observed. This mask is used during
evaluation so that missing future values do not contribute to the reported
error. PhysioNet P12 and MIMIC-III contain sparse clinical measurements with
variable-specific observation patterns. USHCN contains climate variables
observed over long temporal windows, where persistence and seasonal structure
can be strong, while HumanActivity contains denser motion trajectories with a
much longer input and prediction horizon. This diversity allows us to test
whether the Anchor family is effective under local stability while also
revealing when different historical structures favor persistence, smoothing,
sparse repetition, or empirical anchor selection.

\subsection{Baseline Models and Hardwares}

We benchmark the proposed Anchor family against eleven representative baselines from leading venues (e.g., ICLR, AAAI, NeurIPS). Models are divided into two main categories. The first category consists of IMTS classification and imputation methods, including PrimeNet~\citep{PrimeNet}, SeFT~\citep{SeFT}, mTAN~\citep{mTAN}, GRU-D~\citep{GRUD}, Raindrop~\citep{Raindrop}, and Warpformer~\citep{Warpformer}. The second category comprises IMTS forecasting approaches, namely NeuralFlows~\citep{NeuralFlows}, CRU~\citep{CRU}, GNeuralFlow~\citep{GNeuralFlow}, tPatchGNN~\citep{tPatchGNN}, and GraFITi~\citep{GraFITi}. Infrastructurally, Anchor-family experiments are run on a MacBook Pro M2
with 16GB unified memory. Neural-model runs and related measurements are
conducted on a Linux workstation running Ubuntu, equipped with an NVIDIA RTX
5000 Ada Generation GPU with 12,800 CUDA cores, 400 fourth-generation Tensor
Cores, 100 third-generation RT Cores, and 32GB of GDDR6 ECC memory. 





\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

We report masked scaled MAE and MSE. Let $s_v(\cdot)$ be the scaling
transformation fitted on the training split for variable $v$ \citep{apn}. Predictions and
targets are evaluated in scaled space:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{y}_{b,k,v} = s_v(y_{b,k,v}),
    \qquad
    \tilde{\hat{y}}_{b,k,v} = s_v(\hat{y}_{b,k,v}).
\end{equation}

The global masked MAE and MSE is denoted as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{MAE}
    =
    \frac{
        \sum_{b,k,v}
        m_{b,k,v}
        \left|
            \tilde{\hat{y}}_{b,k,v}
            -
            \tilde{y}_{b,k,v}
        \right|
    }{
        \sum_{b,k,v} m_{b,k,v}
    },
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{MSE}
    =
    \frac{
        \sum_{b,k,v}
        m_{b,k,v}
        \left(
            \tilde{\hat{y}}_{b,k,v}
            -
            \tilde{y}_{b,k,v}
        \right)^2
    }{
        \sum_{b,k,v} m_{b,k,v}
    }.
\end{equation}
MAE captures typical forecasting error, while MSE emphasizes large deviations.
This separates irregular health and climate series, where
rare abrupt changes can dominate downstream risk.

\subsection{Benchmarking Protocol}

We follow the published benchmark setting of \citet{apn}, including the public
PhysioNet P12, USHCN, and HumanActivity splits, sequence lengths, prediction
lengths, masks, scaling, and global metric definitions. The current MIMIC
artifacts use two public but non-identical preprocessing adapters: neural models
use the De Brouwer tensor, whereas Anchor methods use the tPatchGNN tensor and
its fixed 60/20/20 split. We therefore report MIMIC for within-adapter
sensitivity but exclude it from controlled cross-model claims. For
selection-based anchors, all choices are fixed before test evaluation. Let
$\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{tr}}$, $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{val}}$, and
$\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{te}}$ denote the train, validation, and test splits.
Prominently, ERMAnchor selects a candidate $a \in \mathcal{A}$ by minimizing non-test scaled
MSE:
\begin{equation}
    a_v^\star
    =
    \arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}}
    \frac{
        \sum_{(b,k)\in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{tr}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{val}}}
        m_{b,k,v}
        \left(
            \tilde{\hat{y}}^{\,a}_{b,k,v}
            -
            \tilde{y}_{b,k,v}
        \right)^2
    }{
        \sum_{(b,k)\in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{tr}}\cup\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{val}}}
        m_{b,k,v}
    }.
\end{equation}

AutoAnchor starts from this empirical-risk selection and applies generic
history-structure rules based on observation density, repeated values, local
stability, and phase-like recurrence. It does not use test labels,
dataset-name-specific parameter tables, or variable-name-specific lookup rules.
After selection, each method is evaluated once on
$\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{te}}$.


