All anchor methods are CPU-only and require no neural training stage. The
wall-clock measurements in Table~\ref{tab:anchor-efficiency} include calibration
and forecasting, so the selection methods pay for their full candidate search.
Even with this accounting, \method{} processes 1,078--3,534 predictions per
second across the four datasets and completes each fixed test split in less than
20 seconds without GPU memory.

\subsection{Anchor-family ladder}

Table~\ref{tab:anchor-family-ladder} decomposes the main anchor result into
standalone and selection-based rules. The ladder shows that the headline result
is not driven by a single accidental baseline: last-value persistence is already
competitive on several variables, exponential smoothing explains much of the
HumanActivity gain, ERM selection improves the clinical datasets, and the full
\method{} rule set gives the largest gains on USHCN. The few metric tradeoffs
are small and informative: on MIMIC, ERMAnchor obtains the best MSE while
\method{} obtains the best MAE; on HumanActivity, ERMAnchor obtains the best MAE
while \method{} ties the best MSE.

