Across the 12 controlled windows in Table~\ref{tab:cross-baseline-lookback},
\method{} has the lowest MSE in 6 settings and the lowest MAE in 9, including
both metrics at the standard window of every matched dataset. It wins three of
four P12 windows on both metrics; GraFITi's advantage at 36/6 is only 0.0005 MSE
and 0.0001 MAE. The pattern is not universal: neural models win USHCN MSE in
three of four windows, although \method{} wins USHCN MAE in three, while
HumanActivity is a near tie with APN. The MIMIC block reports window
sensitivity within each model's own adapter and is deliberately excluded from
these win counts. Thus the controlled evidence is favorable while still exposing
sensitivity to window geometry, metric choice, and preprocessing.

\section{Analysis}

\subsection{Temporal information and history dependence}

Table~\ref{tab:temporal-tests} evaluates whether \method{} uses meaningful
history content or merely benefits from benchmark scaling. Removing the measured
history values while preserving time gaps causes large error increases on every
dataset. Swapping the first and second halves of the history also hurts
substantially, especially on USHCN and the clinical datasets. By contrast,
shuffling timestamps has little effect on P12, MIMIC, and HumanActivity, which
indicates that much of their benchmark performance is explained by value
persistence and local stability rather than precise timestamp geometry.

