\subsection{Synthetic stress tests}

Finally, Table~\ref{tab:synthetic-stress} deliberately constructs cases where
univariate history anchors should fail. Regime shifts, delayed cross-variable
effects, and phase reversals break the anchor assumption. This is the intended
scope of the paper: anchors are a compulsory diagnostic baseline, not a claim
that neural sequence models are unnecessary under distribution shift or hidden
cross-variable dynamics.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Synthetic stress tests. The Oracle row is an unattainable scenario
oracle included only to show the irreducible gap in constructed failure cases.}
\label{tab:synthetic-stress}
\centering
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Scenario & Best anchor rule & Anchor MSE & Oracle MSE \\
\midrule
Stable local & last & 0.0264 & 0.0000 \\
Regime shift & ema03 & 6.4429 & 0.0000 \\
Delayed cross-variable effect & last & 0.5282 & 0.0000 \\
Phase break & trend12 & 0.3169 & 0.0000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Are neural models necessary?}

The careful answer is: not always under current IMTS benchmark protocols. The
Anchor family shows that local history, sparse repetition, smoothing, and simple
non-test selection explain a surprisingly large fraction of performance on the
standard APN-style forecasting tasks. Neural models may still be necessary for
long-horizon distribution shift, cross-variable causal dynamics, and true regime
changes; our synthetic stress tests make this limitation explicit. We therefore
position \method{} as a diagnostic baseline: a neural model should beat strong
anchors before claiming to learn useful irregular temporal dynamics.

\subsection{Implications for benchmarking}

Future IMTS papers should include NaiveAnchor, ExpoAnchor, ERMAnchor, and
\method{} as default baselines. Benchmarks should report performance by history
density, target gap, variable type, and error tail, because aggregate metrics can
hide whether gains come from learned dynamics or from local stability. New
datasets should also include stress conditions where anchoring is insufficient,
such as abrupt regime changes and delayed cross-variable effects.

\section{Limitations}

Current Anchor-family results are deterministic single runs on the fixed APN
protocol; standard deviations would require repeated exposed splits or repeated
benchmark seeds. Anchor methods are also intentionally limited: they do not learn
latent representations, cross-variable causal structure, or anticipatory regime
changes. The strongest interpretation of our results is therefore diagnostic
rather than universal: they reveal that standard benchmark structure rewards
local history anchoring more than many neural-model comparisons acknowledge.

\section{Conclusion}

Simple history anchors are unexpectedly strong for irregular time-series
forecasting. Across four APN-style datasets, the Anchor family provides a
transparent, CPU-only, gradient-free baseline that matches or exceeds several
reported neural baselines and explains when those gains arise from local
stability, sparse repetition, smoothing, or empirical selection. Current IMTS
benchmark conclusions are incomplete without such non-neural anchors, and future
neural methods should demonstrate improvements beyond this stronger diagnostic
floor.

